  "worked_solution": "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{4-3x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3x\\ge 0\\,\u27f9\\,3x\\le 4\\,\u27f9\\,x\\le \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{x+3}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3\\ge 0\\,\u27f9\\,x\\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{3}]\\cap [-3,\\infty )=[\\,-3,\\frac{4}{3}]\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
